---
// Three ecosystems, standard Terraform: a card per ecosystem (well-known providers, collections
// and charts, named nominatively under the home marks note; no competitor), one HCL example each
// (ref-arch chapters 2–4, illustrative), how Turf runs it, then the compatibility ticklist with
// the honesty box. Keeps the `#compatibility` anchor. The reader is as likely to come from a
// console or a GitOps controller as from Terraform (2026-10-21): no migration framing.
const eco = [
  {
    name: 'Terraform', what: 'providers and modules',
    chips: ['AWS', 'Google Cloud', 'Azure', 'Kubernetes', 'Helm', 'Cloudflare', 'GitHub', 'Vault', 'Datadog', 'vSphere', 'Proxmox', 'MAAS', 'Redfish', 'terraform-aws-modules', 'Ansible provider'],
    more: 'the whole registry',
    foot: 'as resources and modules, unchanged',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ansible', what: 'playbooks and collections',
    chips: ['ansible.builtin', 'community.general', 'kubernetes.core', 'amazon.aws', 'google.cloud', 'azure.azcollection', 'ansible.posix', 'community.docker', 'dellemc.openmanage', 'Kubespray', 'k3s-ansible'],
    more: 'your playbooks',
    foot: 'as a Terraform action: ansible-playbook, run from the plan',
  },
  {
    name: 'Helm', what: 'charts',
    chips: ['cert-manager', 'ingress-nginx', 'kube-prometheus-stack', 'Grafana', 'Cilium', 'external-dns', 'Istio', 'Vault', 'GPU Operator', 'Network Operator', 'NIM Operator', 'KubeRay', 'Kubeflow', 'kagent'],
    more: 'any chart',
    foot: 'as a Terraform resource: synced as a true Helm release, hooks included',
  },
];
---

<section class="band alt" id="compatibility">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band-head">
      <h2>Three ecosystems. Standard Terraform.</h2>
      <p>Nothing in a stack is written from nothing. Terraform providers and modules, Ansible®
        playbooks and Helm® charts already cover it, from the hardware to the workload, and Turf runs
        each as it is.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="eco3">
      {eco.map((e, i) => (
        <div class="eco-card">
          <div class="eco-head"><b>{e.name}</b><span>{e.what}</span></div>
          <div class="eco-chips">
            {e.chips.map((c) => <span class="eco-chip">{c}</span>)}
            <span class="eco-chip more">… {e.more}</span>
          </div>
          {i === 0 && (
            <div class="codeblock">
              <div class="cb-head"><span>a module</span></div>
<pre><span class="kw">module</span> <span class="str">"eks"</span> &#123;
  source  = <span class="str">"terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws"</span>
  version = <span class="str">"~&gt; 21.0"</span>
  name    = <span class="str">"gpu-west-1"</span>
&#125;</pre>
            </div>
          )}
          {i === 1 && (
            <div class="codeblock">
              <div class="cb-head"><span>a playbook, as an action</span></div>
<pre><span class="kw">action</span> <span class="str">"ansible_playbook_run"</span> <span class="str">"gpu_node"</span> &#123;
  <span class="kw">config</span> &#123;
    playbooks   = [<span class="str">"gpu-node.yml"</span>]
    inventories = [data.ansible_inventory.gpu.json]
  &#125;
&#125;</pre>
            </div>
          )}
          {i === 2 && (
            <div class="codeblock">
              <div class="cb-head"><span>a chart, as a resource</span></div>
<pre><span class="kw">resource</span> <span class="str">"helm_release"</span> <span class="str">"gpu_operator"</span> &#123;
  repository = <span class="str">"https://helm.ngc.nvidia.com/nvidia"</span>
  chart      = <span class="str">"gpu-operator"</span>
  version    = <span class="str">"v26.7.1"</span>
&#125;</pre>
            </div>
          )}
          <div class="eco-foot"><b>In Turf</b> · {e.foot}</div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="feature">
      <div>
        <h3>Start anywhere. It’s all standard Terraform.</h3>
        <p>Clicked it together in a console? Syncing charts with a GitOps controller today? Turf runs the
          Terraform language on the OpenTofu framework: the whole expression language, the function
          library, registry modules and the provider ecosystem, as they are. Turf is an independent
          product built on the OpenTofu framework, not affiliated with or endorsed by HashiCorp.</p>
        <div class="honesty">
          <b>Radically honest compatibility.</b> Where a construct isn’t supported yet, Turf refuses
          loudly — it never silently mis-plans. Every gap is published and labeled <em>coming&nbsp;soon</em> on the matrix.
          <div style="margin-top:10px;"><a href="/compatibility/">See the full compatibility matrix →</a></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <ul class="ticklist">
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>Full Terraform HCL</b> <span>— every expression and function, no allow
            list.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>Module registry</b> <span>— public, private or git, installed and locked
            by <code>init</code>.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>OpenTofu providers</b> <span>— the whole provider ecosystem,
            unmodified.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>Charts, synced</b> <span>— every <code>helm_release</code> is a true Helm
            release: installed and upgraded through Helm, hooks included, visible to <code>helm list</code>.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>From the console to configuration</b> <span>— the agent writes the HCL;
            an <code>import</code> block brings what exists under management, reviewed as a real diff.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>Terraform actions, with overlays</b> <span>— the 1.16 action model, plus
            triggers added per environment.</span><span class="pill ok">ahead of OpenTofu</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>Multi-environment deploys</b> <span>— one configuration, a workspace per
            environment, promoted with overlays.</span></div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
